Among the most enigmatic of these is *Dickinsonia*, a flat, bilaterally symmetric, ribbed organism up to 40 centimetres long, found on four continents. For decades its biological affinity was unknown: a fungus, a mat, a protist, or an animal? In \citeyear{bobrovskiy2018dickinsonia}, \citeauthorlastnameand{bobrovskiy2018dickinsonia} analysed cholesteroid lipids, specifically the sterol **cholestanol**, preserved in 558-million-year-old *Dickinsonia* fossils from the White Sea in Russia. Cholestanol is synthesized only by animals; its presence in the fossil tissue, in the correct concentration range and absent from the surrounding rock, established *Dickinsonia* as a metazoan. This made *Dickinsonia* one of the earliest known animals, predating the Cambrian Explosion by more than 20 million years.

