## From Foragers to Civilisations: The Sedentarisation That Made Computation Thinkable

The **Neolithic Revolution**, the wide-scale shift from nomadic foraging to settled agriculture beginning around 11,700 years ago, is the largest single change in the human niche since the spread of *Homo erectus*. The very idea that such a prehistoric shift could be called a *revolution* comes from **V. Gordon Childe** (\citeauthor{childe1936man}), who argued in his 1936 *Man Makes Himself* that the British population curve between 1750 and 1800 turns "through an angle of about 30°," and that comparable kinks far enough back in prehistory deserve the same name (\cite[Childe 1936, ch. 1]{childe1936man_txt}). He later labelled two of those kinks the **Neolithic Revolution** and the **Urban Revolution** (\cite[Childe 1950]{childe1950urban}). Without **sedentarisation** (*Sesshaftwerdung*), none of the intellectual and material prerequisites of modern computation could have emerged.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

### From foraging to farming

For the vast majority of their existence, humans were foragers: small, mobile bands that owned few possessions and consumed food within days of gathering it. Australian Aboriginal foragers of the Western Desert, ethnographically studied in the twentieth century, sustained extraordinarily detailed oral traditions for at least 65,000 years — but the lifestyle imposed a hard ceiling on every other dimension of accumulation.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Without stored food, there could be no fixed residence, no pottery, no granaries, no dense craft specialisation, no civic buildings, no writing, no standing armies, no taxation — and no libraries, no schools, and no project that outlasted the working life of a single forager.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

The change began with the climate. As the Pleistocene ice sheets melted, the climate wobbled through a final cold snap (the **Younger Dryas**, ~10,900–9,700 BCE) and then settled into the long, warm, stable interval we still inhabit, the **Holocene**. What made the **Fertile Crescent** special was not a single miracle crop but a *package* of eight wild species — the **Neolithic founder crops** identified by **Daniel Zohary** with Maria Hopf and later Ehud Weiss (\cite[Zohary, Hopf & Weiss 2012]{zoharyhopf2000domestication}): the cereals emmer, einkorn, and barley; the pulses lentil, pea, chickpea, and bitter vetch; and the fibre plant flax.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

The earliest secure evidence for cultivated, morphologically domesticated emmer and einkorn comes from sites in the Fertile Crescent at roughly 9500 BCE, before pottery, before towns, and before writing. Sheep, goats, cattle, and pigs followed; stored grain and managed herds made a year-round sedentary diet possible for the first time.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

### The first towns

Sedentarisation preceded agriculture, not the other way around. The site of **Jericho** (\cite[Kenyon 1957]{kenyon1957digging}), in the West Bank near a perennial spring, has stone-walled occupation layers beginning in the **Pre-Pottery Neolithic A** around 9500 BCE — earlier than full cereal domestication. Its Neolithic tower (c. 8000 BCE, ~8.5 m tall) is one of the oldest stone monuments on Earth, and its surrounding wall and ditch are the earliest known example of **fortification**, a built structure whose purpose is to defend a *fixed place*.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

A few hundred kilometres north, in the Konya plain, **Çatalhöyük** (\cite[Mellaart 1967]{mellaart1967catal}) was occupied from roughly 7500 to 5600 BCE and at its peak housed somewhere between 600 and 8,000 people, the largest known Neolithic proto-city. The second, much longer campaign under **Ian Hodder** (1993–2018) overturned Mellaart's "Mother Goddess" reading: only one of ~2,000 figurines matched the Mother Goddess style, fewer than five percent depicted women, and the houses were remarkably uniform in size with no clear hierarchical elite (\cite[Hodder 2006]{hodder2006leopard}).

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

### The Urban Revolution and the metals

Childe's second coinage, the **Urban Revolution** (\cite[Childe 1950]{childe1950urban}), names what happened around 4000 BCE in southern Mesopotamia: the small farming villages of the Uruk period coalesced into cities. **Uruk** itself grew to perhaps 50,000 inhabitants by 3100 BCE; comparable trajectories played out in Egypt, the Indus valley, the Yellow River basin, and later Mesoamerica.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

In a 1950 *Town Planning Review* paper, Childe listed ten diagnostic features of the first cities — large population, full-time specialists, concentration of surplus, monumental architecture, a ruling class freed from manual labour, systems of recording and writing, exact and predictive sciences, representational art, long-distance trade, and a state organisation based on residence rather than kinship (\cite[Childe 1950]{childe1950urban}) — almost every one of which is a step in the chain that ends at a printed circuit.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Cities needed metal. The **Bronze Age** — Near East c. 3300–1200 BCE — ran on long-distance trade in tin and copper; Childe, in *Man Makes Himself*, makes the deeper argument about why: "The bronze axe which replaces it is not only a superior implement, it also presupposes a more complex economic and social structure. The casting of bronze is too difficult a process to be carried out by anyone in the intervals of growing or catching his food or minding her babies. It is a specialist's job, and these specialists must rely for such primary necessities as food upon a surplus produced by other specialists. Again, the copper and tin of which the bronze axe is composed are comparatively rare, and very seldom occur together. One or both of the constituents will almost certainly have to be imported. Such importation is possible only if some sort of communications and trade have been established and if there is a surplus of some local product to barter for the metals" (\cite[Childe 1936, ch. 1]{childe1936man_txt}).

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

**Iron**, which began to displace bronze around 1200 BCE, was for a long time the *inferior* metal: more abundant, but harder to smelt and cast. Its decisive advantage was *availability*, and the **Iron Age** accordingly democratised the tools bronze had reserved for elites. Childe's principle: each of these shifts is, in his precise sense, a "revolution" — a measurable upward kink in the population curve.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

### Writing, law, libraries

The single most consequential innovation of the Urban Revolution for this lesson is **writing**. It emerged not from literary ambition but from bookkeeping.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

In **Denise Schmandt-Besserat**'s reconstruction, the first system was a set of small shaped clay **tokens** — cones, spheres, disks, cylinders — in use across the Near East from the eighth millennium BCE. Her original 1978 paper in *Scientific American* put the thesis crisply: "Long before the Sumerians invented writing, accounts in western Asia were kept with clay tokens of various distinctive shapes. It appears that the tokens gave rise to the Sumerian ideographs" (\cite[Schmandt-Besserat 1978]{schmandtbesserat1978}; \cite[Schmandt-Besserat 1992]{schmandtbesserat1992}; \cite[Schmandt-Besserat 1996]{schmandtbesserat1996}).

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Tokens sealed inside clay *bullae* were marked on the outside with their own shapes — the first case of a physical record carrying its own verification. Around 3300 BCE, in Uruk IV, the envelope was dropped for a flat tablet on which the token shapes were impressed directly; by 2600 BCE the picture-shapes had been replaced by the wedge-shaped marks we call **cuneiform**, from the Latin *cuneus*.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Cuneiform was the first of five independent inventions of writing: Egyptian **hieroglyphs** (c. 3200 BCE), undeciphered Indus script (c. 2600 BCE), Shang Chinese oracle-bone characters (c. 1250 BCE), and Mesoamerican **Maya** (c. 300 BCE).

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

The next question after cities was how to make a king's word outlast the king. The **Code of Hammurabi** (\cite[Roth 1997]{roth1997lawcollections}; \cite[Scheil 1902]{scheil1902code}) is the earliest preserved answer: a basalt stele ~2.25 m tall, inscribed in Old Babylonian Akkadian around 1753 BCE, rediscovered at Susa in 1901, edited by the Dominican priest **Jean-Vincent Scheil** in 1902, and now in the Louvre.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Its 282 case-form laws cover everything from a surgeon's fee to *lex talionis* — "an eye for an eye." The principle most modern readers remember is the second; the more durable contribution is structural: a body of law, made public on a monument in every major city of the empire, that could be cited after the king's death.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

**Writing** and **coinage**, the two abstractions that let information and value outlive their physical carriers, are the ancestors of every spreadsheet, database, and general ledger an LLM is ever trained on. Where both coexisted, libraries appeared: the **Library of Alexandria** (\cite[El-Abbadi 1990]{elabbadi1990life}; \cite[Canfora 1990]{canfora1990vanished}), founded under the Ptolemies, held between 400,000 and 700,000 papyrus scrolls by the second century BCE — every Greek book that could be found, copied on demand, and held under one roof for cross-reference by Zenodotus, Eratosthenes, Aristarchus, and Hypatia. Its destruction, gradual across several centuries, is one of the great unrecoverable losses of human knowledge.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

### Why sedentarisation is a prerequisite of AI

Strip any one element out of this chain and the rest cannot stand. No agriculture, no fixed residence; no fixed residence, no pottery, no granary, no city; no city, no specialised scribal class; no scribal class, no writing; no writing, no codified law, no coinage, no algebra, no astronomy accurate enough to predict eclipses, no geometry accurate enough to survey after a Nile flood, no library, no encyclopedia, no standard mathematical notation, no "algorithm" in the modern sense.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

Each step was made for reasons that had nothing to do with computation. Yet the chain they collectively built is the chain on which every calculator, database, operating system, and large language model rests. A language model is a model of human *language*, and human language was *first made durable* by the scribes of Sumer.

</div>

<div class="md" data-depth="80">

The grammatical categories of Akkadian and Sumerian, the case system of Latin, the inflectional morphology of ancient Greek, the tonal layers of Middle Chinese, the analytic structure of Old English — all of these are inside the corpus on which an LLM is trained, because they were inside the libraries that writing built, that agriculture made possible, that the cities of the first urban revolution sustained. Take the chain away and the corpus thins to oral memory; take the oral memory away and the model has nothing to model.

</div>

